---
import PlaygroundShell from '../../components/PlaygroundShell.astro';
import RangeField from '../../components/RangeField.astro';
---
<PlaygroundShell title="Back-of-the-envelope numbers" hint="How many timestamps the oracle must give out, how long a repository takes to rebuild in batches compared with updating it incrementally, and how much extra work incremental transactions cost. A teaching model with round numbers, not Google's data.">
  <div class="pg est" data-est>
    <div class="pg-row">
      <RangeField label="Updated documents each second (thousands)" name="u" min="1" max="100" step="1" value="10" />
      <RangeField label="Documents in the repository (billions)" name="r" min="1" max="100" step="1" value="10" />
      <RangeField label="Hours to run the whole batch chain over the repository" name="m" min="1" max="24" step="1" value="6" />
      <RangeField label="Bigtable operations in one plain write" name="t" min="1" max="5" value="1" />
      <RangeField label="Percolator overhead on a write (times)" name="o" min="2" max="8" step="1" value="4" />
      <RangeField label="Seconds Percolator takes to process one updated document" name="p" min="1" max="30" step="1" value="2" />
      <RangeField label="Timestamp requests batched into one call" name="l" min="1" max="100" step="1" value="10" />
    </div>
    <dl class="est-out" data-out aria-live="polite"></dl>
    <p class="pg-note">Teaching model, not Google's system or data. From the Percolator paper: every transaction asks the timestamp oracle for two timestamps, the oracle serves around 2 million timestamps a second from one machine thanks to batching, Percolator adds roughly a factor of four overhead to a write in the paper's benchmark, and the incremental system processed a new document in about 2 seconds against about 30 minutes on average for the batch chain at a low crawl rate. The update rate, the size of the repository, the time of the batch chain and the batching are my guesses that you can change. Assumptions: each update is one transaction, a batch run processes the whole repository every time, the delay a document waits for the batch is half the run plus the run itself, and updates are spread evenly.</p>
  </div>
</PlaygroundShell>
<script>
  import { query, numberValue, formatOneDecimalBelow100 } from '../../playground';
  document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-est]').forEach((root) => {
    const q = query(root);
    const val = numberValue(q);
    const OUT_ROWS = 10; // lines in the result list
    const rows = Array.from({ length: OUT_ROWS }, () => { const dt = document.createElement('dt'); const dd = document.createElement('dd'); q('[data-out]').append(dt, dd); return { dt, dd }; });
    const f1 = formatOneDecimalBelow100;
    const pct = (n: number) => `${(Math.round(n * 1000) / 1000).toLocaleString('en-US')}%`;
    function draw() {
      ['u', 'r', 'm', 't', 'o', 'p', 'l'].forEach((key) => (q(`[data-${key}val]`).textContent = String(val(`[data-${key}]`))));
      const rate = val('[data-u]') * 1000; const docs = val('[data-r]') * 1e9; const batchHours = val('[data-m]'); const plain = val('[data-t]');
      const overhead = val('[data-o]'); const seconds = val('[data-p]'); const batching = val('[data-l]');
      const stamps = rate * 2;
      const data: [string, string][] = [
        ['Timestamps the oracle gives out each second (2 per transaction)', f1(stamps)],
        ['Share of the oracle\u2019s 2 million a second', `${f1((100 * stamps) / 2e6)}%`],
        ['Calls to the oracle each second, with batching', f1(stamps / batching)],
        ['Bigtable operations per write, with Percolator', f1(plain * overhead)],
        ['Average delay before a new document is processed, in batches (minutes)', f1(batchHours * 60 * 1.5)],
        ['Average delay with incremental processing (seconds)', f1(seconds)],
        ['How many times faster incremental processing is', `${f1((batchHours * 60 * 1.5 * 60) / seconds)} times`],
        ['Documents the batch run reprocesses to add one new document (billions)', f1(docs / 1e9)],
        ['Documents incremental processing touches for one new document', 'one, plus the ones it triggers'],
        ['Documents a day that incremental processing handles at this rate (billions)', f1((rate * 86400) / 1e9)],
      ];
      rows.forEach((r, i) => { r.dt.textContent = data[i][0]; r.dd.textContent = data[i][1]; });
    }
    root.addEventListener('input', draw);
    draw();
  });
</script>
